---
name: job-hunter-daily-scan
description: Daily autonomous job-hunter pipeline: scan boards, pre-score/JD-fetch/ATS-score only new roles, tailor CVs for matches above threshold, write dated report, push summary notification.
---

<!--
  This is a TEMPLATE. Copy it into your own Claude Code scheduled task
  (or run it manually as a prompt) and fill in every [PLACEHOLDER] before
  use. Everything else is the reusable pipeline structure — don't remove
  steps, just fill in the bracketed parts with your own details.
-->

You are running the daily morning job-hunter scan for [YOUR NAME], using the "job-hunter" MCP server tools configured in the project at [ABSOLUTE PATH TO YOUR CLONE] (plus your email tool for steps 3 and 12a, and your task-manager tool for step 12). Follow this pipeline exactly, in order.

Note on Option B (auto-submit via browser): this scan never drives a browser to submit applications — that requires your live, in-the-moment confirmation per role and is done in an interactive session afterward, not in this unattended run. Whenever an application is actually sent off (portal submit or emailed), call `update_application_status(role_id, "applied")` immediately — this must happen every time, not just be inferred from a task-tracker item being checked off.

**Option B execution notes** (for the interactive follow-up session where you ask Claude to walk through submitting an application):
- Claude should use its browser-automation tools right away for this — no need to ask permission first if you've already granted it standing approval for job-application work.
- If a login page already has username/email and password filled in (browser autofill or you pre-filling them), Claude should click the login/sign-in button directly rather than asking you to log in yourself first — if you've granted that approval.
- File-upload tools for browser automation can be unreliable across different MCP/browser integrations — if one fails, have Claude ask you to attach the CV/cover-letter PDFs manually rather than retrying repeatedly.
- If you're keeping a memory of standing screening-question answers, have Claude check it before asking you to repeat yourself — but verify which exact field a standing answer maps to when a form has two similarly-named fields (don't pattern-match on label similarity alone).

Background you need: duplicate roles are blocked automatically at the database layer (same company + same title within a configurable window is treated as the same vacancy even under a different URL). Roles from "full JD" boards (Greenhouse/Lever/Ashby/etc.) still land at status='listed' and must clear the same cheap title-only pre-score gate as everything else before full ATS-scoring reasoning is spent on them — don't bypass this.

1. Call `import_job_boards_from_file` to pick up any manually-added boards.
2. Call `scan_all_boards`. Separate results into: succeeded with roles found, succeeded with zero roles found (flag as "check manually — may be broken"), and explicit errors (flag as "failed — needs manual paste"). [Optional: list any boards you've deliberately excluded from automated scanning here, e.g. because you get email alerts instead, or because they consistently fail to scrape and you check them by hand.]
3. **Email ingestion** [optional — remove this step if you don't want it]: search your inbox for job-alert emails received since the last run (~24-30 hours) from job-board senders (e.g. LinkedIn, Indeed, Glassdoor). Extract every listing mentioned (title, company, URL, location, posted_at if stated) and call `add_manual_roles` with the batch. Note counts/issues in the report.
4. Call `filter_stale_listings` (no args — reads config). Mechanically zeroes pre_score on 'listed' roles with a known posting date older than 14 days that aren't in your exempt location. Note the count filtered.
5. Call `list_roles` with `status="listed"` and `needs_pre_score=true` — genuinely new, non-stale-filtered roles never triaged before.
6. If there are new roles, call `get_master_cv` for your CV text, and call `get_scoring_criteria` for the full filters.yaml content — this includes `location_rules`, `remote_work_criteria`, `employer_exemptions` (read any employer-specific guidance in full and apply it precisely, not just the pre-score bypass line), and `cover_letter_guidance`. [YOUR BACKGROUND HERE — write 3-5 sentences summarizing your career background, core domain expertise, and any standout differentiators (languages, certifications, unusual combinations of experience) the scorer should weigh. This is read by the scoring step every run, so keep it accurate and current.]
7. Pre-score every new role from step 5 (title/company/location only). Score 0-100 on title/domain relevance (main driver) and seniority signal (adjust the seniority band description to your own level — e.g. "Director/GM/Head/VP scores high; junior/intern scores under 15"). Apply `location_rules` for on-site roles (strict outside your home city, flexible within it) and `remote_work_criteria` for confirmed-remote roles (career-path fit doesn't matter there — score on remote-eligibility, pay, interest, rough skill overlap instead; exclude gig-economy call-center/microtask/rewards platforms per the exclude list). Apply `employer_exemptions`: any role at an exempted employer bypasses the normal domain-fit bar entirely — only exclude if clearly intern/student-level. Call `store_pre_scores` with the batch, with a short `notes` rationale per role (mention which rule drove the decision — location, remote, or employer exemption).
8. Call `promote_pre_scored_roles` (reads pre_score_threshold from config). Advances full-JD roles that cleared pre-score straight to status='new'. Note the count.
9. Call `get_config` to read `pre_score_threshold`. Call `list_roles` with `status="listed"` and `min_pre_score` at that threshold. Call `fetch_job_descriptions` with those IDs. For each entry in `failed_roles`, check its `error` message: if it indicates the posting has closed/been removed, call `update_application_status(role_id, "closed", notes=error)` so it stops being re-fetched on every future run instead of accumulating as a permanent daily failure — this matters especially for any employer exempted from the staleness filter in step 4, since those postings never age out any other way. For any other failure reason, leave it in `listed` and report it under "JD fetch failures" as before (needs manual paste).
10. Call `list_roles` with `status="new"`. For any, call `get_role_details_bulk` (batch ~15-20 at a time) and score each on the 5-dimension ATS model (required_skills 40, seniority 25, experience 20, education 10, preferred 5) against your master CV. Apply `location_rules`/`remote_work_criteria` again now that the full JD is available. **Critically, apply `employer_exemptions` guidance during this full-scoring step, not just at pre-score** — use whatever seniority-band/domain-transfer/education/department guidance you've written into filters.yaml for your priority employers, and make sure it's actually followed here, not just referenced. Call `store_scores` with the batch.
11. Call `get_config` to read `score_threshold` (default 70). For each employer, check whether `employer_exemptions` in filters.yaml defines a `score_threshold_override`. Call `list_roles` with `status="scored"` and pull everything meeting either the normal threshold or, for exempted employers, their override threshold. For each qualifying role: call `get_role_detail` for the full JD, then write **two separate Markdown documents — a CV and a cover letter. Never combine them into one file.** The CV markdown should contain only resume content (header, professional experience with proper blank-line-separated bullet lists, education, certifications, skills — no letter prose). The cover letter markdown should contain only the letter (salutation, body paragraphs, sign-off — no resume content).

   **Rendering convention (mandatory — never use freeform bold text for names or job titles)**: `cv_engine/renderer.py` only applies correct styling when the Markdown follows this exact structure:
   - Candidate name as `# Name` (H1) — NOT `##` — with the contact-info line as the very next paragraph (gets small-gray-text styling automatically).
   - Every job/education entry as `### Title` (H3) immediately followed by one paragraph in the exact form `Company/Institution – Location | Date range` (pipe-separated) — never `**Title**` as inline bold. The renderer parses this exact shape into italic company/date styling; anything else falls back to one run-on paragraph.
   - Always leave a blank line between that company/date line and the bullet list beneath it — Markdown treats bullets with no preceding blank line as plain-text continuation of the prior paragraph, producing an unreadable wall of text with literal hyphens instead of real bullet points.
   - Cover letter: the subject line naming the role (e.g. "Application for {Title} — {Department}") must be a `##` heading, not plain body text, so it renders as a visually distinct highlighted subtitle.

   Getting this wrong produces a broken-looking PDF (name renders as an all-caps section header, title/company/date jam together, near-blank trailing pages) — check the output, don't assume it's right.

   **Apply `cover_letter_guidance`**: per the `general_principles`/employer-specific guidance in filters.yaml (mission/impact motivation alongside career logic, explicit long-term-location commitment where true, explicit bridging of the specific transferable skill for this role). As always: reorder/reframe/compress your ACTUAL master-CV content only — NEVER invent skills, employers, titles, or experience not present in the master CV, and never fabricate genuine engagement with a specific document you haven't referenced. Call `store_tailored_cv(role_id, cv_markdown, cover_letter_markdown)` — it renders both to separate PDFs.

11a. **Mandatory verification (do not skip)**: after calling `store_tailored_cv`, Read both returned PDF paths back and visually check them — name header styled correctly (H1, not an all-caps section header), each entry has distinct title/company/date lines (not run together), bullet points render as real bullets, the cover letter's subject line is a visually distinct highlighted subtitle, no orphaned near-blank trailing pages — before considering that role's documents done. Fix and re-render immediately if anything looks off (most common cause: missing a blank line before a bullet list, or not using the `### Title` + `Company – Location | Dates` two-line convention) — don't defer this to a later interactive session, and don't treat a successful tool call as proof the output is good.

12. **Task-tracker creation** [adapt to whatever task manager you use — Todoist, Things, a plain markdown checklist, etc.]: for every role from step 11, create a task with content = "Apply: {Company} — {Title}", due date = today, description with ATS score, job URL, and both the CV PDF and cover letter PDF paths. Note if unavailable.
12a. **Email outreach drafting (optional — Option C)**: for every role from step 11, check whether the JD text or the company's own careers page states a direct application email address (never guess or reuse an email seen for a different role/company).
   - **Verify before using**: the address must appear on the employer's own official domain/JD text, not a third-party aggregator. If verification is inconclusive, skip the draft and note it in the report rather than guessing.
   - **Draft, never send**: create a draft email addressed to the verified email, subject "Application: {Title} — [YOUR NAME]", a short 2-4 sentence professional body (interest + one-line bridge to the role, note CV/cover letter attached), with both PDFs from step 11 attached. Never send automatically — you send manually.
   - Record the draft's presence and the **exact source of the email address** (JD line or careers-page URL it came from) in both the task-tracker entry and the report table.
   - If no application email exists anywhere (portal-only application), note "portal-only — no email path" instead of fabricating a generic address like info@/hr@.
13. Compile a markdown report at `[YOUR REPORTS PATH]/<today's date, YYYY-MM-DD>.md`:
    - **Boards needing attention** (excluding intentionally-removed ones)
    - **Email ingestion** counts/issues
    - **Stale roles filtered** count
    - **JD fetch failures** with instructions to paste manually
    - **New roles found today** by source, plus duplicate_skipped counts
    - **Roles ready to apply to** — table (Company | Title | ATS Score | Threshold Used (normal/employer override) | Missing Skills | CV PDF path | Cover Letter PDF path | Job URL | Task created? | Email draft (source, or "portal-only")), sorted by score descending
    - [Optional: a per-priority-employer status section, the way the AIIB section worked in the original — how many roles exist total, how many are pending, how many were scored today, whether any reached an override threshold. Worth adding for any employer you're specifically tracking.]
    - **Recurring missing-skills themes** across all scoring today, distinguishing genuine CV gaps from structural mismatches not worth chasing
    - **Pipeline summary** via `get_pipeline_overview`
    - **Run errors** if any
14. Send a push notification [or however you want to be pinged]: new roles found (scrape vs. email), roles ready to apply today, and any manual-attention items. Under 3 sentences.

If a tool call fails unexpectedly, don't retry indefinitely — note it in "Run errors" and continue with available data. Always write the report file even if some steps failed.
